Fixtures for the browser's host tests.

links.html / list.html / media.html / table.html
    Small hand-written pages for the layout and paint tests.

baidu-ua-stub.html  (227 bytes)
baidu.html          (697,700 bytes)
    THE SAME URL, https://www.baidu.com/, fetched twice on 2026-08-08 with
    nothing different but the User-Agent header. They are the evidence for the
    "the browser fetches a page and then shows nothing" bug, and
    tests/unit/loader_test.c asserts on both.

    baidu-ua-stub.html is what OUR User-Agent gets:

      curl -H 'User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; LogitOS x86_64) Logit/1.0' \
           -H 'Accept: */*' https://www.baidu.com/

    227 bytes: an inline <script> calling location.replace() to downgrade
    https -> http, plus a <noscript> containing a meta refresh. No stylesheet,
    no <script src>, no image, no visible text.

    baidu.html is what a browser-shaped User-Agent gets over the same request:

      curl --compressed \
           -H 'User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 \
                           (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/120 Safari/537.36' \
           https://www.baidu.com/

    697 KB: 2 stylesheet <link>s, 11 <script src>, 20 <img>, and the hot-search
    list. Stored DECODED -- the wire body is chunked gzip, and what the parser
    is under test for starts after Content-Encoding is undone.

    The header that decides which one you get is ONLY the User-Agent; Accept
    and Accept-Encoding make no difference (checked by holding each fixed and
    varying the other). The same 227-byte stub over http:// instead of https://
    returns the full 696 KB page, which is why the site's own redirect is
    correct and following it is our job.

    Refreshing these: baidu's markup changes daily, so a re-capture will move
    the counts in loader_test.c's CONTROL checks. They are asserted as ">=" for
    that reason; the stub has been stable and is the part the tests turn on.
